---
intent: >
  Implement the ancestor chain, description field, and status field enhancements
  to the discover tool so that deep-mode calls return the full intent lineage
  from root to target before the subtree, with each ancestor showing its
  description and non-default status.
---

## Plan

### ~~1. Add `description` and `status` to AideFrontmatter type~~ ✓

**File:** `src/types/index.ts`

- Add `description?: string` to the `AideFrontmatter` interface — a one-line purpose
  statement read from frontmatter.
- Add `status?: "aligned" | "misaligned"` to `AideFrontmatter`. The value `"pending"`
  is the implicit default represented by the absence of the field — it is never stored
  or surfaced.
- Add a new `"missing-description"` literal to the `ValidationWarning.kind` union.
  This is the warning kind the validate tool will use when a spec lacks a description.

**Why:** The type layer must exist before any reader or renderer can reference these
fields. Playbook convention: types are centralized in `src/types/` (folder-structure
note). `status` is typed as an optional union of the two explicit states, not a
three-value enum, because `pending` is the absence of a value — storing it would
violate the spec's "implicit default" rule.

---

### ~~2. Parse `description` and `status` in parseFrontmatter~~ ✓

**File:** `src/util/parseFrontmatter/index.ts`

- In the `coerceFrontmatter` function, add extraction for `description` (string) and
  `status` (validate against `"aligned" | "misaligned"`, ignore any other value).
- If the raw YAML `status` field is present but not one of the two valid values,
  silently drop it (do not error, do not store `"pending"`).

**Why:** `parseFrontmatter` is the single gateway for YAML extraction — all consumers
(discover, read, validate) get the new fields for free once it parses them. Playbook
convention: no horizontal imports between tools; shared logic lives in util
(folder-structure note, dependency flow rules).

---

### ~~3. Build the ancestor chain helper~~ ✓

**Creates:** `src/tools/discover/buildAncestorChain/index.ts`

This is a new helper subfolder under `discover/`, following the orchestrator/helper
pattern (progressive-disclosure doc, conventions note).

- **Default export:** `buildAncestorChain(root: string, targetPath: string): Promise<string>`
- **Behavior:**
  1. Starting from `targetPath`, walk upward directory by directory toward `root`.
  2. At each directory level, check for `.aide` or `intent.aide` (in `.aide/` for
     root level — the project root spec lives at `.aide/intent.aide`).
  3. For each spec found, read the file contents and call `parseFrontmatter` to
     extract `description` and `status`.
  4. Collect results into an ordered array from root down to the target's own
     directory (excluding the target dir itself — those specs are in the subtree).
  5. Render as a vertical lineage list (not a tree). Format per node:
     `  <relativePath> — <description>` with ` [aligned]` or ` [misaligned]`
     appended after description when status is set.
  6. If a spec has no description, show the path alone with no em-dash (do not
     fabricate from intent or summary).
  7. Return the full block prefixed with `Ancestor chain:\n`, or empty string if
     no ancestors exist (e.g., the target is the project root).

- **JSDoc:** Explain that this walks from target up to root collecting .aide specs
  at each directory level, then renders top-down so agents read broadest context first.

- **Edge cases:**
  - Root-level spec at `.aide/intent.aide` must be found even though it is inside
    a subdirectory of root, not at root itself. The walker should check for
    `.aide/intent.aide` when visiting the root level.
  - A directory may have `.aide` OR `intent.aide`, never both (validated elsewhere).
    Check `.aide` first, fall back to `intent.aide`.
  - If `targetPath` equals root, return empty string (no ancestors above root).

**Why:** The spec requires the ancestor chain to appear before the subtree in
deep-mode responses. It is a distinct rendering concern from `buildTree` (which
renders the subtree). Extracting it as its own helper keeps both renderers focused
and independently testable. Playbook convention: every helper gets its own subfolder
with `index.ts` and default export (conventions note, folder-structure note).

---

### ~~4. Wire ancestor chain into the discover orchestrator~~ ✓

**File:** `src/tools/discover/index.ts`

- Import `buildAncestorChain` from `./buildAncestorChain/index.js`.
- In the deep-mode branch (when `path` is provided), call `buildAncestorChain(root, path)`
  before building the subtree.
- Prepend the ancestor chain output (if non-empty) before the tree in the final
  response string. Separate ancestor chain from subtree with a blank line.
- The final output shape for deep mode becomes:
  `header\n\nancestorChain\n\ntree\n\nwarnings` (each block present only when
  non-empty).

**Why:** The spec is explicit: "ancestor chain FIRST — then the detailed subtree."
The orchestrator coordinates the two helpers in sequence. Playbook convention:
orchestrators coordinate, helpers compute (progressive-disclosure doc).

---

### ~~5. Update the MCP tool description for aide_discover~~ ✓

**File:** `src/index.ts`

- Update the `description` string for the `aide_discover` tool registration to
  mention the ancestor chain behavior: when called with a path, the response opens
  with the ancestor chain showing the cascading intent lineage from root to target,
  each with its description and alignment status, followed by the subtree detail.
- Update the `path` parameter description to mention the ancestor chain.

**Why:** The spec says "the tool's description and output shape teach agents the
cascading intent protocol by example." Agents read the tool description before
calling it — it must accurately describe what they will receive.

---

### ~~6. Add `missing-description` validation to the validate tool~~ ✓

**File:** `src/tools/validate/index.ts`

- After the existing broken-link checks, add a new check: for each scanned `.aide`
  file, read its content, call `parseFrontmatter`, and warn if `description` is
  missing or empty. Use kind `"missing-description"`.
- The frontmatter is already being read for the broken-link check; reuse that read
  rather than reading the file twice. Parse frontmatter from the already-loaded
  content and check `frontmatter.description`.

**Why:** The spec says `description` is required on all specs. The validate tool is
where structural health checks live. This makes missing descriptions discoverable
via the existing `aide_validate` workflow.

